  "lessons": "- **Substantive Indigenous, Canadian, and community civic stories** remain accepted (First Nations advocacy, reconciliation, land stewardship, local services, community grants); routine sports recaps and event listings stay rejected.\n- **Environmental coverage** is accepted when framed around solutions, protection, policy stakes, or industry pivots; pure weather or disaster reporting is mostly rejected unless high-impact or policy-relevant, while climate-driven weather pattern analysis (e.g., El Ni\u00f1o effects) can land well.\n- **Climate and health intersections** are accepted when analysing population or chronic-illness effects or policy accountability; health-politics coverage centred on individual public figures is rejected.\n- **EV and automotive** coverage is accepted for corporate strategy, charging infrastructure, battery breakthroughs, and electrification trends; model reveals, spec reviews, luxury showpieces, and listicles are rejected.\n- **Science and nature discovery** is strongly favoured: surprising natural phenomena, biology, astronomy, geoscience, and physics with concrete implications; pseudoscience and symptom-driven or wellness-checklist health advice are rejected.\n- **AI and tech** is accepted for security vulnerabilities, hacks, surveillance controversies, legal rulings, and critiques of AI hype or techno-solutionism; product launches, gadget news, and executive-deal coverage are mostly rejected, though practical tech how-tos and business-model analysis can succeed.\n- **US domestic politics and partisan commentary** are rejected, including voting-rights and political-figure-centred pieces, unless framed around broader policy or land/public-resource stakes.\n- **Consumer, home, and lifestyle content** (decor, styling, product roundups, bargain guides, celebrity home t",
